//
// calls.md `@FR-F-Block` — a block's value is discarded *"only where the BLOCK itself is a
// statement — a `;`-terminated one"* — and `@FR-F-Drop` adds that the work still runs.
// Nothing in either rule says which SIDE of an `if` the discarded value sits on, so both
// orders are statements and both must compile.
//
// Only one of them did.  A void THEN arm makes the expected type `void`, which accepts any
// else arm; a void ELSE arm arrived at the arm-agreement gate as a conversion `void ⤳
// integer`, which nothing licenses.  So `{ println } else { 5 };` compiled and its mirror
// `{ 5 } else { println };` was refused — on both backends, while `match`, which reaches the
// same gate, accepted both.
//
// The fix keys on POSITION, which only the caller knows: `parse_block`'s statement loop is
// what sees the `;`.  Two things make that harder than it looks, and both are pinned below:
//
//   * a leading `if` does not prove statement position — a function TAIL begins its statement
//     too, and there the value is the function's, so its arms must still agree.  The gate
//     therefore RECORDS the mismatch and the loop reports it unless a `;` followed.
//   * `Type::Void` on an arm is not one fact: it is also what a block reports when its value
//     travels through a BUFFER, so a rule keyed on the arm type alone drops those deliveries.
//     Position AND a void arm is the pair that is safe.
//
// @falsified-at: 964bab93 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0 (the parent build
//   refuses the mirror at parse time, so nothing below runs there).
//   HOLDS: on --interpret, assertions, leak, panic, free-refusal, expectations are equal on
//   both trees, measured by `scripts/falsify.sh --bulk` against 964bab93; falsify prints only
//   the channels that MOVE, and requires HERE to be clean, so each of those is clean on both.
//   The exit channel carries this one on that backend. The native side was not scored in that
//   run.

fn main() {
  k = 2;
  ran = 0;

  // The order that already compiled.  `k != 1`, so the VALUE arm runs and yields nothing
  // anybody reads.
  if k == 1 { ran = ran + 100 } else { 5 };
  assert(ran == 0, "the value arm's result is discarded, ran={ran}");

  // Its mirror — the defect.  Here the VOID arm is the one that runs, and `@FR-F-Drop` says
  // its work still happens.
  if k == 1 { 5 } else { ran = ran + 1 };
  assert(ran == 1, "the void arm's work still runs, ran={ran}");

  // Both arms values, in statement position: discarded, and the work runs.
  if k == 1 { 5 } else { 7 };

  // Both arms void.
  if k == 1 { ran = ran + 100 } else { ran = ran + 1 };
  assert(ran == 2, "both-void statement if, ran={ran}");

  // An `else if` chain with a void arm in the middle.
  if k == 1 { 5 } else if k == 2 { ran = ran + 1 } else { 9 };
  assert(ran == 3, "an else-if chain with a void arm, ran={ran}");

  // A `match` in statement position, both orders — it reaches the same gate.
  match k { 1 => { ran = ran + 100 }, _ => { 5 } };
  match k { 1 => { 5 }, _ => { ran = ran + 1 } };
  assert(ran == 4, "a statement match, either order, ran={ran}");

  // A VALUE-position `if` is unaffected: both arms are values and it produces one.
  v = if k == 1 { 5 } else { 7 };
  assert(v == 7, "a value if still produces its value, v={v}");

  // A value `if` NESTED inside a statement `if` does not inherit statement position:
  // its arms are still held to each other, and it still yields.
  w = 0;
  if k == 2 { w = if k == 1 { 3 } else { 8 }; }
  assert(w == 8, "a nested value if still yields, w={w}");

  println("1382 OK");
}
